well the title of the book is money and  banks in the American political system  and I decided to write it because I was  living in Washington and I was actually  working on another book about the IMF  and the World Bank and the American  Congress and the financial crisis  happened and what I found as a result of  that was that there was a pretty big gap  in the literature just for anyone who is  educated and wanted to follow the crisis  about how and why different institutions  were responding so why was the Federal  Reserve doing this why would Congress  involve with the tarp why was the  president involved or not involved and  then we had an election and things  changed again when new appointees came  in so because of that experience I  decided to try to put it all and draw a  map in a one accessible place where  people could understand looking to the  future how the political system works in  this area and how the agencies do what  they do well so much of the book is  about how our American political culture  infuses this system with good and bad  things that have made it possible to  have the vibrant dynamic financial  services sector that we have but at the  same time the political system has been  a source of crises and so there's a good  side to it and a bad side and so the  book starts with a historical review of  the of the development of the American  financial system going back to the  American Revolution and paying off the  debt of the Revolution and then our very  interesting history with central banks  and I say interesting relative to other  advanced industrial democracies so  that's the the first segment but then  during the civil war the government had  to pay for the costs of the war with a  financial system that was pretty  unsophisticated relative to what else  existed and so we started to have a  national banking system in the civil war  that that exists alongside the state  banking system so the point here is that  the same things that fuel American  politics in other areas what does the  state get to do versus what does the  federal government get to do so  obviously in the Civil War the big  question was slavery but there were  other questions as well about who would  issue money who would regulate banks all  of these same questions  were present in American politics and  they have it just like the issues of  slavery haven't gone away and the  residual effects of the Civil War  haven't gone away in that area they  haven't gone away when it comes to banks  either what happened in the American  experience is that the United States  industrialized in such a way that the  major American corporations were out  there before the regulatory agencies  appeared so you think about major  corporations United Fruit even Standard  Oil and the major banks Citibank being  one of them they actually existed before  the regulatory reforms of the Great  Depression in many countries are our  peers our advanced industrial peers they  were much more in tandem when they  developed so in our country we've always  had a lack of comfort with the  centralization of power in our governing  institutions and also in our banking  institutions and as everything else in  the United States is contested between  the state and the federal government  it's no different in banking and some  areas of the United States were much  more comfortable with large banks that  operated internationally so that would  be the Northeast New York State  obviously being the preeminent example  and then in the south there was much  more hesitancy and reluctance about the  role of banks and even more reluctance  out west so early in American history  some western states outlawed banking  entirely the the reserved fractional  banking system so when the Federal  Reserve was created it was a compromise  between people who wanted the government  to control the central bank so we have a  Board of Governors in Washington and  then people who wanted to have more  control by bankers out in the region so  we have a system of regional central  that a regional Federal Reserve Bank  which we have one here in Cleveland  there's one in Atlanta you know and so  on so Chicago so on and so on  so this was a compromise at the time but  it compromised it didn't work very well  because everybody was making policy so  during the Great Depression that system  was altered and and we now have a  centralized monetary policy system in  the the FOMC that's located in  Washington but until that time you know  the Federal Reserve had  like to these different characteristics  well like you pointed out the SEC is a  creature of a different era the Great  Depression and the collapse of the stock  market so at that moment in American  history it was perceived that we needed  to regulate securities markets up until  that time it had only been regulated at  the state level and so creating a whole  system of a securities regulator with a  completely different notion of how to  regulate the idea with with the  securities market says let the buyer  beware  so give people transparent information  and let them decide whether or not they  want to buy this stock and if you lose  your shirt it's it's your own fault but  that's a very different philosophy than  bank regulation which is to try to  prevent a catastrophe from occurring  then we have other agencies like the  Office of the Comptroller of the  currency so that's a part of the  Treasury Department that regulates banks  that's a product of the Civil War so the  whole necessity of regulating banks the  perception when we when we established a  national system of banks so these  different agencies have always responded  to different constituencies and they  regulate different banks so the  additional agency that you can see why  you needed a map for this so the  additional agency that we that is very  important as the FDIC the Federal  Deposit Insurance Corporation which is  also a creature of the Great Depression  and the notion that people need to have  Deposit Insurance so they won't there  won't be a run on the bank because they  know that their money will be there well  the FDIC has different interests in the  political system because it regulates  different banks but also if the banks  that are regulated by the OCC or the  Federal Reserve fail the FDIC has to pay  out on those so it tends to be a much  more conservative regulator because it  doesn't want to have to spend its entire  insurance fund bailing out the banks  that are regulated by other regulators  so these different agencies come from  different eras but they also have  different purposes based on what role  they form but also what banks they  regulate I don't know if it's helpful  doubt but I kind of think of it like if  you want to think of an analogy to a  person this might be overly simplified  but you say well I have  um I to write checks so that's the  Treasury the Treasury is the the part of  the system that pays out for the  government and taxes people so it takes  money in but it the Federal Reserve is  the bank so that would be like you know  around here would be Huntington Key Bank  Chase one of the one of the banks where  you would you would keep the money on  deposit now  the Treasury can't do anything unless  Congress tells them it's okay so it's  you can't write the checks without the  Congress telling you so if you kind of  think about this relationship the head  of the Federal Reserve has some  functions as the head of the the bank  that that would naturally tend to  conflict with the Treasury Department  and like we've pointed out because these  institutions have been worked out at  different moments in American history  some of the relationships are not very  clear so we were saying a lot of it  comes down to politics and the politics  depends on the individuals who hold the  jobs that they have and one thing I find  fascinating is that it doesn't  necessarily reflect their political  parties so you've had incredibly  positive relationships between  Republican appointees and I think the  famous example is Alan Greenspan at the  Federal Reserve having a great  relationship with Robert Rubin at the  Treasury in the Democratic Clinton  administration and Alan Greenspan in his  own accounts would say that he didn't  have the same good working relationship  with some of the Republican appointees  that had preceded secretary Rubin so you  don't necessarily have you know you had  good relationships with Paul Volcker at  the Federal Reserve with some  Republicans and not so much with some  Democrats and vice versa because Volkers  policies really put the economy into a  recession which was damaging for some  Democrats so it isn't necessarily a  partisan relationship I think what  people have pointed to since Ben  Bernanke was at the head of the Fed is  that some Federal Reserve chairs are  very good at going up on the hill and  talking to people and making clear to  the American public I know that's Ben  Bernanke's aim to try to open up and be  mystify the Fed and let them understand  why it does what it does and  burn it up but they've all had their own  styles and their own ability some people  are better at working with others and  also it depends on whether or not they  think that monetary policy is the  appropriate way to handle whatever the  crisis is or the secretary of the  Treasury might think that fiscal policy  is or is not the the best way you had a  shift in in policy in American  government between the federal  government wanting to control the  economy through taxing and spending  policy shifting to having the federal  reserve play a larger and a larger role  in moderating the economy so if we go  back to previous recessions there had  been the sense that in the George W Bush  administration we had almost mastered  the the recession the business cycle  that we had gotten so good at it  that the Federal Reserve was so able to  handle things that that we really  weren't going to have a major collapse  again and interest rates were low some  people would argue that the Federal  Reserve kept interest rates too low for  too long but people went into housing  markets for a variety of reasons and  again here you're going to get a variety  of explanations from across the  political divide about what was going on  but there was an undue perhaps emphasis  on housing and people were looking for  higher rates of return on their on their  deposit accounts and on their  investments and housing provided higher  and higher rates of return as housing  markets moved to people who had worse  and worse credit histories so you tend  to pay a higher rate of interest when  you have a worse credit rating now the  financial services industry again  depending on on what your view of the  industry is whether responding to a  consumer need or acting responsibly  depending again on where you sit on a  political spectrum but the financial  services industry responded by providing  products that provided higher interest  rates and some of these were highly  risky and when they collapsed they were  so complicated  that it was unclear who actually even  was responsible for the mortgage that  was ultimately at the endpoint and when  you had a massive collapse in such an  important segment  the American financial services market  you had the threat that the whole system  will come down so you started to see  some hedge funds early on collapse the  Bear Stearns and and then you started  you had runs on the commercial paper  market leading up to 2010 / 2008 events  and then in September 2008 you started  to have massive collapses of major  institutions well in a major reform  legislation that came out of 2008 was  the dodd-frank reform act that tried to  change the worst practices of the system  so that we wouldn't have another  financial crisis so one of the more  obvious results was the creation of the  Consumer Financial Protection Bureau so  we have a new agency that was created  and the idea here was that there would  be an emphasis on the consumer in the  financial services marketplace so when  people go out to make a home loan they  have a good understanding of what  they're actually borrowing and what  their obligations are so when they go  into the rest of the financial services  marketplace they understand when they're  taking on risk when they're engaging  volatility because sometimes we want  more risk and volatility but it's  important for the consumer to understand  when they're taking that risk on or  maybe if it's with retirement money and  they don't want it to understand when  they are not so that has been one new  area that's opened up and and even in  areas like home loan or excuse me  student loans and some other things  we've seen important initiatives that  have come out of that agencies the other  big area has to do with the Volcker Rule  so trying to rein in some of what people  perceive to be the most risky practices  of some of the major banks in terms of  trading securities on their own book or  are they providing services for  customers or they are you know trying to  bring back the spirit of the old  glass-steagall where banks do what banks  do and the investment banking industry  does what the investment banking  industry does the my goal in writing the  book was not to place emphasis on any  one policy prescription for the future  my goal was to give people an  understanding of the entirety of the  system so that they could understand the  components as they relate to each other  and they could make up their own mind  about whether or not they think the  emphasis should be put on stopping  campaign contributions from Wall Street  or should the emphasis be on the comment  period when the agencies are making  rules should that be the area that they  should wear they should make their mark  should they join an interest group  should political parties where do they  think in the system would be the most  effective place to press for real change  and not just express frustration with  the system but really be able to  understand who is it when we say the  government does something the government  doesn't do anything some agency or some  part or a presidential administration  there is a component of the government  that's actually acting but it's helping  or harming you where is it identify it  and come up with solutions because  that's the only way that that's that's  the way banks operate and that's the way  financial services industry operates so  the only way that you could you could  address those issues is by working  through the same channel and so I hope  to take away is for people to understand  themselves where the where they see the  problem and be able to go forward and  and work within those constraints

